# Build context = the REPO ROOT (both services use `context: ..`), so everything here is sent to
# the daemon on every build, and anything a Dockerfile COPYs becomes a cache layer.
#
# This is an ALLOW-list on purpose. The previous version was a deny-list of four entries
# (node_modules, target, dist, .git) and it missed `frontend/.angular`, the Angular build cache —
# which grew to 6.1 GB locally. `COPY frontend/ .` then wrote a 6.55 GB layer, and because the
# cache changes on every local `ng build`/`ng test`, every docker build produced a NEW 6.5 GB
# cache entry instead of reusing one. Four builds in a day = 26 GB; that is how the build cache
# reached 117.5 GB.
#
# A deny-list only protects against the junk someone thought of. An allow-list means the next
# tool that invents a multi-gigabyte cache directory cannot silently end up in an image layer.
# If a Dockerfile needs a new path, re-include it here explicitly.

# 1. Exclude everything.
*

# 2. Re-include only what the two Dockerfiles actually COPY.
#    frontend.Dockerfile: frontend/package*.json, frontend/, docker/nginx.conf,
#                         docker/security-headers.conf
#    backend.Dockerfile:  backend/pom.xml, backend/src
!frontend
!backend/pom.xml
!backend/src
!docker

# 3. Strip build artifacts and caches back out of the directories re-included above.
#    These are regenerated inside the image; shipping them is pure waste and, worse, they change
#    on every local run and so bust the COPY layer's cache hash every single build.
frontend/node_modules
frontend/.angular
frontend/dist
frontend/.vscode
frontend/.impeccable
backend/target

# 4. Never useful in an image, and noisy for cache invalidation.
**/.DS_Store
**/*.log
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
